 ### [Tile-Based Culling](2819-tile-based-culling.md)
-Computes a precise mapping between 3D Gaussians and image tiles to eliminate irrelevant tiles during rasterization. It sets itself apart from the parent method by iteratively examining candidate tiles to verify actual overlap rather than assigning a Gaussian to every tile its projected splat touches, reducing radix sort workload. Choose it when conservative bounding boxes cause excessive overdraw.
+Computes a precise Gaussian-to-Tile mapping in 3D Gaussian Splatting to cull irrelevant tiles during rasterization. It sets itself apart from siblings like [SnugBox](3378-snugbox.md) by using a simpler control flow with warp-level load balancing, and from [AccuTile](2109-opacity-thresholded-tile-intersection-filtering.md) by iterating over all candidate tiles within the bounding box rather than using a non-iterative precise algorithm. Choose it when conservative bounding boxes cause excessive overdraw.
 
 ### [Tile-based Rasteriser](2846-tile-based-rasteriser.md)
-A GPU-optimized rendering component that sorts projected 2D Gaussian splats once per frame to serve both forward rendering and backpropagation. It sets itself apart from per-pixel sorting methods by dividing the image into tiles and using a single global radix sort to achieve per-tile depth ordering. Choose it for real-time performance with millions of splats.
+The core rendering component of 3D Gaussian Splatting that sorts projected 2D Gaussian splats once per frame to serve both forward rendering and backpropagation. It sets itself apart from per-pixel sorting, which is too slow for real-time performance, by achieving parallel depth resolution and compact per-tile lists. Choose it for real-time performance with millions of splats.
 
 ### [Gaussian Rasterization Key Generation](5133-gaussian-rasterization-key-generation.md)
-Generates the key-value pairs required for tile-based depth sorting in 3D Gaussian Splatting. It sets itself apart from filtering methods by explicitly generating the full set of overlaps for sorting rather than just discarding them. Choose it as the direct input stage for the global radix sort.
+Generates key-value pairs for all Gaussians overlapping with tiles, enabling spatial sorting by tile ID and depth. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like [Tile-Based Culling](2819-tile-based-culling.md) by explicitly generating the full set of overlaps for sorting rather than just filtering, and from [Tile-based Depth Sorting](8038-tile-based-depth-sorting.md) by focusing on the key construction phase rather than the sorting algorithm itself. Choose it as the direct input stage for the global radix sort.
